        "problem": "| **Temperature,\nin  ${}^{\u2218}F\\,(x)$** | $60$ | $42$ | $80$ | $70$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Humidity, in $%\\,(y)$** | $42$ | $24$ | $30$ | $60$ |\n\nThe table above shows data Tom collected about temperature and humidity in his city. \nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{\u2015}{x}=63$ and $\\overset{\u2015}{y}=39.$",
        "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},\u2026,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},\u2026,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{\u2015}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{\u2015}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{\u2015}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{\u2015}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{\u2015}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{\u2015}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(60\\,-\\,63)(42\\,-\\,39)+(42\\,-\\,63)(24\\,-\\,39)+(80\\,-\\,63)(30\\,-\\,39)+(70\\,-\\,63)(60\\,-\\,39)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(3)+(-21)(-15)+(17)(-9)+(7)(21)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-9+315-153+147] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 300 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
